    "case_summary": "A 47-year-old woman, with a history of diabetes and hypertension, visited the urology clinic in July 1995. She reported experiencing gross intermittent blood in her urine and painful urination for the past year. Additionally, she had symptoms of urgency and urge incontinence. Approximately 25 years earlier, she underwent a laparotomy for intestinal obstruction caused by tuberculosis and completed a one-year course of antituberculous therapy. Physical examination revealed some fullness in the left lower quadrant of the abdomen. Laboratory results showed hemoglobin at 8.5 gm/dl and hematocrit at 26.2% (normal range: 35.4 – 42.8%), with normal coagulation profile and routine chemistry. Urinalysis indicated the presence of blood and pus cells, but urine culture was negative for bacterial growth. Ultrasound imaging identified a 2.6 × 1.8 cm polypoidal mass on the left posterior wall of the urinary bladder along with bladder wall irregularity. An intravenous pyelogram (IVP) demonstrated normal upper urinary tracts but revealed a filling defect in the bladder.\n\nCystoscopy revealed an approximately 4 × 3 × 3 cm solid tumor in the left postero-lateral wall, above the ureteric orifice. Multiple biopsies of the tumor from the base and edges, as well as random bladder biopsies, were obtained. The tumor was arranged in large clusters with diffuse sheets invading and insinuating in between the smooth muscle fibers. The tumor cells exhibited pleomorphism, nuclear hyperchromasia, with 2–3 mitoses/10 HPF. Random bladder biopsies were normal. Immunohistochemical studies showed the tumor cells to be S-100 positive and cytokeratin Cam 5.2, AE/A13 negative.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
